<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion language models (DLMs) offer parallel sampling for faster generation than autoregressive models, an increasingly important advantage as inference becomes a dominant computational workload. However, DLMs degrade quickly with fewer sampling steps, process the full sequence at every step, and require many passes over the dataset to reach good quality. We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Next-Concept Modeling (NCM)</span>, a two-stage, coarse-to-fine framework: a multiscale residual VQ-VAE encodes text into a hierarchy of discrete codes, and a transformer generates one level at a time, predicting all codes within a level in parallel. At matched parameter scales, <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> achieves better generative perplexity than the strongest DLM baseline on LM1B while running <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="21\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster. On OpenWebText, <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> is competitive with the strongest DLM while running <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="10\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster; on XSum, it matches the strongest DLM’s ROUGE-L while being <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="31\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>31</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster. On the reasoning task of Sudoku, <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> outperforms autoregressive and diffusion baselines across all difficulty levels, using fewer function evaluations.</p>
  
