<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion language models (DLMs) are the leading non-autoregressive language
modeling paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Their main
appeal is their ability to produce many tokens at a time through iterative
denoising, which can make generation substantially faster than in autoregressive (AR) models that generate one token at a time. This is becoming increasingly important, as language model inference is one of the dominant computational workloads <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. DLMs are moving from research to practice: open models
such as LLaDA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> scale to billions of parameters, commercial
systems such as Mercury <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and DiffusionGemma
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> serve diffusion models in production, and
diffusion drafters like DFlash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> accelerate AR models through
speculative decoding.</p>
</div>
