<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background &amp; Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion &amp; Flow-Based Models for Text Generation.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Text generation using Diffusion &amp; Flow-Based Models is an iterative refinement
process. Discrete methods operate on token sequences
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>; continuous methods evolve
representations of tokens in an embedding space
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
Distillation/flow-map techniques enable reduction of the number of sampling steps
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>; however, full-sequence
formulations still require processing a length-<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> representation in every step.
Block-Autoregressive Diffusion allows KV caching through sequential block
generation: tokens within a block are generated in parallel; however, each block
must be generated after the previous blocks
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> modifies both the representation
and the generation schedule. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> generates a learned hierarchy of discrete
codes from coarse to fine, and predicts all codes within each level in parallel.
Generation therefore follows a short sequence of levels, with fewer positions
processed at the early, coarse levels.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Discrete latent variables and next scale prediction.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A number of image generative models use discrete tokenizers
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Residual quantization
corrects for errors made at previous steps and uses those errors to create a new
codebook <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> which has been adapted to neural audio codecs
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. Next-scale
prediction generates such hierarchies coarse to fine, one scale at a time in parallel, for
fast, high-quality images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> extends residual
quantization and next-scale prediction to text through a hierarchy of discrete
codes that determines the generation order. While latent diffusion models generate
continuous representations of text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>,
<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> produces successive discrete levels. Adapting this approach to text requires
learning useful coarse representations without a natural counterpart to image
downsampling. Accurate reconstruction is also important: a single token error can
alter meaning, prevent code from running, or invalidate a Sudoku solution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hierarchical and concept-level text generation.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several approaches organize language modeling around larger units and learned
abstractions. MEGABYTE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>
combines global modeling of byte patches with local byte prediction, while
H-Net <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> learns dynamic chunks
within a multistage hierarchy. Large Concept Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>
predict sentence representations. ConceptLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> predicts discrete
concepts to guide subsequent token generation, and Dynamic Large Concept
Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> learn variable-length concepts for computation in a
compressed representation space. HDLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> progressively refines
tokens through a hierarchy of semantic vocabularies. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> represents an entire
sequence through discrete levels of increasing resolution and generates these
levels in order using a shared transformer. Predicting all codes within each
level in parallel gives a short generation path, while the shorter coarse levels
reduce computation during early steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Next-Concept Modeling</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> generates text through a learned coarse-to-fine hierarchy of discrete codes.
We first train a multiscale residual VQ tokenizer to represent text at progressively
finer levels of detail, then freeze it and train a transformer to generate the hierarchy.
At each level, the transformer predicts all codes in parallel, conditioned on the
cumulative reconstruction of earlier levels. The tokenizer’s decoder maps the
completed reconstruction back to text. Figure <a href="#S1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>
previews this process through a German–English translation, showing how the
output develops across levels.
The term <em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concept</em> here refers to the information carried by the coarse
levels of the hierarchy.</p>
