<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conditional generation.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> supports prompt-conditioned generation through a prefix-LM attention pattern. Prompt tokens are embedded using the frozen VQ-VAE encoder’s token embedding table and projected into the hidden space. Prompt positions attend to each other; each target block attends to the prompt and itself, while prompt positions cannot attend to target blocks.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> along four axes:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quality.</span> We first assess tokenizer quality through reconstruction accuracy and codebook utilization (§<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Tokenizer Quality ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). We then evaluate unconditional generation on TinyStories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, One Billion Word (LM1B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, and OpenWebText (OWT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, measuring generative perplexity alongside sample diversity (§<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Unconditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). For conditional generation, we evaluate WMT14 German–English translation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> using BLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> and XSum summarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> using ROUGE-1/2/L <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> (§<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Conditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency.</span> We compare the number of function evaluations (NFE) and wall-clock time required to achieve a given generation quality, as well as the training-token budgets used to obtain these results (§<a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6 Efficiency ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning.</span> We compare <span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> with autoregressive and diffusion baselines on Sudoku, where successful generation requires satisfying interdependent constraints across the entire output (§<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Reasoning ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scaling.</span> We investigate how scaling the model size improves generation quality and whether <span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>’s fast sampling can translate into further quality gains through additional test-time compute (§<a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Scaling ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across these evaluations, <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> achieves competitive generation quality with substantially lower sampling cost.
We train a separate tokenizer and generator for each dataset. Unless otherwise stated, our generators have approximately <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>M parameters, comparable to or smaller than the baselines. We follow established evaluation protocols from prior diffusion language modeling work where possible. Appendices <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Datasets ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>–<a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Additional Results ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> provide dataset, architecture, training, and baseline details, along with additional results.</p>
</div>
