<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reconstruction errors that are visually imperceptible in images can be consequential in text: e.g., one wrong digit can invalidate a Sudoku solution. Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Tokenizer Quality ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports exact reconstruction accuracy. On Sudoku, where correctness requires exact recovery, the tokenizer achieves <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> reconstruction accuracy. On other tasks, like summarization and translation, token-level reconstruction accuracy is close to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Observed tokenizer reconstruction errors often involve substitutions of related words or entities, with their effect on meaning depending on the context. We also report tokenizer codebook utilization in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Tokenizer Quality ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, with mean utilization ranging from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> across datasets. Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Tokenizer Analysis ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a> provides further tokenizer analysis, including reconstruction errors and codebook utilization.
We provide additional experiments in Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Probing the Hierarchy ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a> to further
understand the learned hierarchy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Unconditional Generation</h3>

<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.14162v1/efficiency.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/143;" width="476" height="143"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Unconditional generation quality and sampling cost.<span id="S4.F5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Generative perplexity (Gen. PPL; lower is better) against the number of function evaluations on TinyStories and LM1B, and against the sampling time per <math id="S4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}024</annotation></semantics></math>-token sequence (batch size <math id="S4.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>) on OpenWebText. </span>NCM<span id="S4.F5.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> achieves competitive or better Gen. PPL compared to strong diffusion baselines with substantially fewer function evaluations and less time. <math id="S4.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> denotes mean per-sample unigram entropy.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following standard practice in the diffusion language modeling literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, we evaluate <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>’s unconditional generation quality using generative perplexity (Gen. PPL), with GPT-2-large as the reference model on TinyStories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, LM1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, and OpenWebText (OWT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. We report mean per-sample unigram entropy (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>) alongside Gen. PPL, since lower Gen. PPL can be obtained by reducing sample diversity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. For reference, we report both metrics on ground-truth text from each dataset. For a fair comparison, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> is compared against baselines with sample diversity close to that of the ground-truth data. Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 Unconditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compares <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> with strong discrete and continuous diffusion language model baselines in terms of Gen. PPL at different numbers of function evaluations (NFE). Across the three datasets, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> achieves competitive or better Gen. PPL with substantially fewer function evaluations, while maintaining sample entropy close to that of the data. Table <a href="#A5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ E.2 Unconditional Generation ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Unconditional Generation ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a> lists all results with their entropies, and Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Generative Perplexity Under Other Reference Models ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a> gives results under other reference scoring models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Conditional Generation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> on translation (WMT14 German–English, De–En) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> and extreme summarization (XSum) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, using BLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> and ROUGE-1/2/L <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> scores, respectively. We compare <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> with autoregressive baselines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and diffusion and flow models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> at comparable model sizes (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Conditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Without guidance, <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> achieves better ROUGE-1/2/L scores on XSum than the strongest diffusion baseline, AURORA-LM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, while reducing the number of function evaluations (NFE) by <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="16\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times</annotation></semantics></math>. Among the non-AR baselines on WMT14, <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> trails only the strongest diffusion baseline, E2D2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, while using <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5.8\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.8\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer NFE. <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>’s lower sampling cost allows us to match the baselines’ NFE budget using multiple candidates and minimum Bayes risk (MBR) decoding. <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> with MBR achieves better scores on all three XSum ROUGE metrics than the strongest diffusion baseline with guidance, AURORA-LM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, while using fewer NFE (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Conditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). We count each classifier-free guidance step as two function evaluations, since it requires both a conditional and an unconditional generator call. We visualize generated samples in Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.7 Qualitative Samples ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Additional Qualitative Samples ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>.</p>
</div>
