</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> on Sudoku, a reasoning task in which every cell of the solution must satisfy the row, column, and box constraints at once. Following the setup of S-FLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, we report the percentage of completely solved boards at three difficulty levels defined by the number of provided clues (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>35</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> for easy, medium, and hard, respectively). <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> outperforms both the AR baselines and the strongest diffusion baseline, FMLM+ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, across all three levels of difficulty while using fewer NFE (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.4 Reasoning ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>’s coarse-to-fine generation is well suited to tasks that require global planning: <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> refines a representation of the entire board across levels, allowing later levels to correct earlier partial solutions. In contrast, the AR baselines struggle with left-to-right generation, which commits to individual tokens without revising earlier decisions. Appendix <a href="#A10.SS7" title="J.7 Sudoku ‣ Appendix J Additional Qualitative Samples ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J.7</span></a> shows a sample Sudoku solving trajectory across different time steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Scaling</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study how <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> benefits from additional compute through larger models and increased inference budgets. We evaluate model scaling by increasing generator size and test-time scaling by selecting among multiple candidates using minimum Bayes risk (MBR) decoding.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model Scaling.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scaling <span id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>’s model size improves both generation quality and sample diversity at a fixed training-token budget (Figure <a href="#S4.F6.sf1" title="Figure 6(a) ‣ Figure 6 ‣ Test-Time Scaling. ‣ 4.5 Scaling ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(a)</span></a>). We train generators with 97M, 317M, and 725M parameters on LM1B, all sharing the same frozen tokenizer. These results suggest that <span id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> scales effectively, with potential for further gains from larger generators.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Test-Time Scaling.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Conditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we show that <span id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> matches or outperforms diffusion baselines while using significantly fewer function evaluations (NFEs) and less wall-clock time. A natural question is whether we can convert these efficiency gains into further improvements in quality. We generate multiple candidates and use minimum Bayes risk (MBR) decoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> to select the candidate with the highest average similarity to the others. On XSum, Figure <a href="#S4.F6.sf2" title="Figure 6(b) ‣ Figure 6 ‣ Test-Time Scaling. ‣ 4.5 Scaling ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(b)</span></a> shows that <span id="S4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> improves over diffusion baselines at matched inference time.</p>
</div>
