<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Efficiency</h3>

<section id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inference Efficiency.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 Unconditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we show that <span id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> achieves lower or comparable generative perplexity than the strongest diffusion baselines using far fewer function evaluations (NFE). However, NFE alone does not capture the full inference efficiency picture, since the cost per step differs across different diffusion language models; many DLMs process the full sequence at every step and use samplers that can introduce additional overhead. <span id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>, on the other hand, processes very few tokens at the coarse levels of its hierarchy, saving compute at these early steps. We benchmark end-to-end wall-clock time for generating <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}024</annotation></semantics></math> sequences at batch size <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> on a single NVIDIA H100 SXM 80GB GPU (Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). On LM1B, <span id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> uses <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="11\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer NFE than the strongest diffusion language model baseline, DUO, and runs <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="21\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster at better generative perplexity. On XSum, it uses <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="16\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer NFE than the strongest diffusion baseline, AURORA-LM, and runs <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="31\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>31</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster at comparable ROUGE. The speedup gains come from both fewer NFE and a lower average cost per step compared to the DLM baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_sectional-block ltx_minipage">
          
<section id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Lower Training Budgets.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> achieves strong unconditional generation results with fewer training tokens than the diffusion baselines. On LM1B, the tokenizer and generator together process <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13.36" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13.36</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13.36</annotation></semantics></math>B full-length token positions, approximately <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4.9\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.9\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer than the baselines’ <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>66</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">66</annotation></semantics></math>B (Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Inference Efficiency. ‣ 4.6 Efficiency ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). We count repeated passes over the data in this budget; the calculation is given in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Generator (NCM) ‣ Appendix C Architecture Details ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>. These budgets measure total token exposure. One possible explanation is that <span id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>’s fixed coarse-to-fine order simplifies learning relative to the many generation orders learned by diffusion baselines.</p>
</div>
