<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">N</span>ext-<span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>oncept <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>odeling (<span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>), a
two-stage framework that first learns a discrete coarse-to-fine representation
of text and then trains a transformer to generate its levels in order.
Predicting all codes within each level in parallel provides a short sequential
generation path. On unconditional generation across TinyStories, LM1B, and
OpenWebText, <span id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> achieves competitive or better Gen. PPL than strong diffusion
baselines with approximately <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="11\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer function evaluations (NFE),
while maintaining sample entropy close to the data. Generation is
<math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="21\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster on LM1B and OpenWebText. On conditional generation,
<span id="S5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> uses approximately <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="16\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer NFE than unguided diffusion
baselines on WMT14 translation and XSum summarization, achieving competitive
translation quality and the best summarization scores. On XSum, it runs
<math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="31\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>31</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than the strongest diffusion baseline. It also uses
approximately <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer training tokens than strong diffusion
baselines on LM1B and OpenWebText, including both tokenizer and generator
training. Finally, preliminary scaling results on LM1B show that increasing
the generator size improves generation quality at a fixed training-token budget.</p>
</div>
