<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Baselines</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix states where every baseline number in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> comes
from and how the baselines were sampled.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Unconditional Generation</h3>

<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LM1B.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All LM1B baselines (RDLM, CANDI, MDLM, DUO, and FLM) use the checkpoints of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. [15]</a></cite>, who trained them for the same <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>M iterations on <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>66</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">66</annotation></semantics></math>B tokens. We
sample each with <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\{8,12,16,32,64,128\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\{8,12,16,32,64,128\}</annotation></semantics></math> steps under the protocol of
Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Unconditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. RDLM is omitted from Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 Unconditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> because its
Gen. PPL lies far above the other curves.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">TinyStories.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train the AR, DUO, MDLM, and CANDI baselines ourselves using the same
transformer architecture and training settings as the TinyStories <span id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> generator
(Table <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.2 Generator (NCM) ‣ Appendix C Architecture Details ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). For FMLM+, we use the publicly released
checkpoint <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">OpenWebText.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MDLM, DUO, SEDD, and Eso-LM baselines use publicly available
checkpoints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>.
We sample each with <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\{8,16,\ldots,1{,}024\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>024</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\{8,16,\ldots,1{,}024\}</annotation></semantics></math> steps under the same protocol.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Conditional Generation</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MDLM, DUO, E2D2, and SeqDiffuSeq results of Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Conditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
are taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. [13]</a></cite> (marked <sup id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>), with their
configurations. We use publicly available checkpoints of ELF (with and without
guidance) and AURORA-LM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> to reproduce their quality results
and measure sampling time under the setup of Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6 Efficiency ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>.
For conditional baselines without released checkpoints, we benchmark sampling
time using randomly initialized weights with the same model architecture and
task-specific sampling configuration reported for those tasks. These runs are
used only for timing; their quality results are taken from the published results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Reasoning</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow the Sudoku evaluation setup of S-FLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>,
reporting the percentage of completely solved boards at three difficulty levels:
easy, medium, and hard, with <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>35</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> provided clues, respectively.
The baseline results in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.4 Reasoning ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> are taken from
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al. [49]</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional Results</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix checks that the LM1B comparison does not depend on the scoring model
(Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Generative Perplexity Under Other Reference Models ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>) and collects unconditional generation results from
Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Unconditional Generation ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Generative Perplexity Under Other Reference Models</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To check whether the LM1B comparison depends on the reference model, we evaluate the same checkpoints and sampling settings as Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 Unconditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> under four additional autoregressive models. Table <a href="#A5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ E.1 Generative Perplexity Under Other Reference Models ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> averages five sampling seeds with <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> samples each. <span id="A5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> has the lowest average Gen. PPL, using approximately <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer training tokens and <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> sampling steps rather than <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
