<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Ablations</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We ablate the key design choices in <span id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>: the latent code hierarchy
(Appendix <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Latent Code Hierarchy ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>), the NCM transformer
(Appendix <a href="#A6.SS2" title="F.2 NCM Transformer ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a>), code corruption
(Appendix <a href="#A6.SS3" title="F.3 Code Corruption ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a>), and sampling (Appendix <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Sampling ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a>).
Unless otherwise stated, we run our ablations on TinyStories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.
Each experiment changes one design choice and keeps every other setting fixed.</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Latent Code Hierarchy</h3>

<section id="A6.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">F.1.1 </span>Number of Levels</h4>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The number of levels <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> sets the number of generation steps. We compare ladders on
TinyStories with <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\{4,8,16,32\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\{4,8,16,32\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each uses a linear schedule starting at <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and
ending at <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each ladder has its own tokenizer with <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4{,}096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4,096</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4{,}096</annotation></semantics></math> codes per level, trained for <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math>k
steps. We then train an NCM transformer on top of each tokenizer for <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>k steps.
We do not use code corruption, so the ladder is the only meaningful difference between
runs. We compute Gen. PPL using <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math> generated samples and multiple sampling seeds,
with GPT-2-Large as the reference model. Reference text has Gen. PPL <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9.67</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.67</annotation></semantics></math> and
unigram entropy <math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=3.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=3.96</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.14162v1/levels_ablation.svg" id="A6.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/172;" width="476" height="172"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A6.F9.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A6.F9.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation on the number of levels in the hierarchy.<span id="A6.F9.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
</span>(a)<span id="A6.F9.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Gen. PPL against training steps.
</span>(b)<span id="A6.F9.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Gen. PPL at the final checkpoint.
</span>(c)<span id="A6.F9.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Cumulative reconstruction accuracy per level.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Gen. PPL decreases as the number of levels in the hierarchy grows
(Figure <a href="#A6.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Setup. ‣ F.1.1 Number of Levels ‣ F.1 Latent Code Hierarchy ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). It continues to improve at the largest hierarchy tested,
but with diminishing returns: further increases in the number of levels yield smaller
gains. Panel (c) shows cumulative reconstruction accuracy; longer hierarchies
achieve slightly better final reconstruction, although all four reconstruct at least
<math id="A6.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99.95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.95\%</annotation></semantics></math> of tokens. These ablations do not use code corruption, so errors can accumulate
across levels; adding corruption may narrow the gaps between hierarchies.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">F.1.2 </span>Level Schedule</h4>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The level schedule determines how the number of codes grows from coarse to fine.
We consider six schedules: linear, quadratic, square-root, logarithmic, exponential, and
cosine. Each has <math id="A6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=12</annotation></semantics></math> levels, starting at <math id="A6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> code and ending at <math id="A6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>; code lengths are
rounded to integers. Figure <a href="#A6.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ F.1.2 Level Schedule ‣ F.1 Latent Code Hierarchy ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> compares Gen. PPL over training,
Gen. PPL of the final checkpoint at <math id="A6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>k steps, and the schedules themselves.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.14162v1/hierarchy_shapes.svg" id="A6.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/174;" width="476" height="174"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A6.F10.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A6.F10.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation on the level schedule.<span id="A6.F10.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
</span>(a)<span id="A6.F10.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Gen. PPL against training steps.
</span>(b)<span id="A6.F10.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Gen. PPL of the final checkpoint at <math id="A6.F10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>k steps (bars).
</span>(c)<span id="A6.F10.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Code lengths for six level schedules.
<math id="A6.F10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the final per-sample unigram entropy.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>NCM Transformer</h3>

<section id="A6.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">F.2.1 </span>Attention Pattern</h4>

<div id="A6.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison purposes on TinyStories, we used the exact same <math id="A6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>-level tokenizer,
generator, and selected conditioning-corruption recipe (mean rate <math id="A6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.50</annotation></semantics></math>;
Appendix <a href="#A6.SS3" title="F.3 Code Corruption ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a>) as in the previous example. The input blocks at each
level are aggregated from the earlier levels. Block-causal attention also includes
information from earlier blocks; a two-level window includes the preceding block.
Block-diagonal attention is the best option for generating low PPL and fast sampling
time with good preservation of entropy (Table <a href="#A6.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ F.2.1 Attention Pattern ‣ F.2 NCM Transformer ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). We therefore
use this in <span id="A6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>.</p>
</div>
