<section id="A6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.3 </span>Code Corruption</h3>

<figure id="A6.F13" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:38%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.14162v1/corruption_ablation.svg" id="A6.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:17.662em; height:13.922em;aspect-ratio:235/185;" width="235" height="185"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A6.F13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 13</span>: </span><span id="A6.F13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of code corruption.<span id="A6.F13.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Gen. PPL after <math id="A6.F13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>k steps vs. mean corruption rate. Nonzero rates use a shifted linear decreasing
schedule; <math id="A6.F13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is entropy.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During training, <span id="A6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> uses teacher forcing and conditions on clean input codes;
during inference, it conditions on its own generated codes. This difference creates
opportunities for errors to accumulate across levels. Since codes within each level
are generated in parallel, multiple errors can enter the reconstruction used to
generate the next level. We address this mismatch by making the model resilient to
incorrect input codes: during training, we replace some input codes with entries
sampled uniformly from the same level’s codebook, at a different rate per level.
Targets remain clean.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This ablation and the loss-weighting ablation (Appendix <a href="#A6.SS2.SSS3" title="F.2.3 Loss Weighting ‣ F.2 NCM Transformer ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2.3</span></a>) use
TinyStories (<math id="A6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math> tokens) and the <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>-level tokenizer of
Table <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ C.1 Multiscale VQVAE Tokenizer ‣ Appendix C Architecture Details ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Each <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="88.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>88.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">88.6</annotation></semantics></math>M <span id="A6.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span> transformer is trained for
<math id="A6.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> steps with a batch size of <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}024</annotation></semantics></math>. Gen. PPL is averaged over five
sampling seeds with <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> samples each.
The degree of corruption matters: moderate corruption greatly improves generation
quality, while excessive corruption weakens the conditioning signal and degrades
quality (Figure <a href="#A6.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ F.3 Code Corruption ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>). The type of
corruption, including how rates vary across levels and which replacement codes are
selected, also affects how closely training resembles generation. We use a linearly
decreasing corruption rate across levels. Corruption based on the model’s actual
errors may better match inference conditions; we leave this to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.4 </span>Sampling</h3>

<div id="A6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The quality of <span id="A6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NCM</span>’s generations may be enhanced during inference time without
retraining. The two types of decoding choices we examine in
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Conditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> are Classifier-Free Guidance and Minimum Bayes Risk (MBR)
Decoding.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classifier-Free Guidance.</span>
For Classifier-Free Guidance, we use VAR’s approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> to allow
for Classifier-Free Guidance in multiscale generations by combining conditional
and unconditional logits from each scale using a guidance weight <math id="A6.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. As
illustrated in Figure <a href="#A6.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ F.4 Sampling ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, Classifier-Free Guidance results in less
improvement on XSum summarization compared to WMT14 translation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F14" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.14162v1/guidance.svg" id="A6.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/146;" width="452" height="146"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A6.F14.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 14</span>: </span><span id="A6.F14.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of classifier-free guidance.<span id="A6.F14.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> De–En BLEU and XSum
ROUGE-1/2/L as a function of the guidance weight <math id="A6.F14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. All three ROUGE metrics
peak at <math id="A6.F14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\!=\!0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.128em">=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\!=\!0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, the setting
reported in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Conditional Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>; BLEU, which is length-corrected, peaks later
at <math id="A6.F14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\!=\!1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.128em">=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\!=\!1.0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Minimum Bayes Risk Decoding.</span>
We draw <math id="A6.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> candidates and keep the one with the highest average similarity to
the rest. Figure <a href="#A6.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ F.4 Sampling ‣ Appendix F Ablations ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> sweeps <math id="A6.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>: all three metrics rise monotonically
with diminishing returns, gaining <math id="A6.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.48</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.48</annotation></semantics></math> ROUGE-1 in total from <math id="A6.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\!=\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\!=\!1</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="A6.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\!=\!256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\!=\!256</annotation></semantics></math>.
The main-table settings use <math id="A6.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=16</annotation></semantics></math> on XSum (<math id="A6.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math> NFE) and <math id="A6.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=12</annotation></semantics></math> on WMT14
(<math id="A6.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="132" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>132</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">132</annotation></semantics></math> NFE), yielding <math id="A6.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>29.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29.6</annotation></semantics></math> ROUGE-L and <math id="A6.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25.2</annotation></semantics></math> BLEU, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F15" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.14162v1/mbr.svg" id="A6.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/146;" width="452" height="146"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A6.F15.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 15</span>: </span><span id="A6.F15.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of MBR candidate count.<span id="A6.F15.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> De–En BLEU and XSum
ROUGE-1/2/L against the number of sampled candidates <math id="A6.F15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, on a log-spaced axis.
Quality improves as more candidates are sampled. </span>NCM<span id="A6.F15.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">’s low sampling cost allows
multiple candidates within the NFE budget that diffusion baselines use for a single
sample, enabling extra inference compute to be used for sampling and verification
through MBR selection.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
