<section id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tokenizer Failure Cases.</h5>

<div id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate typical tokenizer reconstruction errors using the OWT tokenizer as a
case study. As reported in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Tokenizer Quality ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it achieves <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.99\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99.99</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.99\%</annotation></semantics></math> exact token
reconstruction accuracy, corresponding to approximately <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01\%</annotation></semantics></math> incorrectly
reconstructed tokens. Table <a href="#A7.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Tokenizer Failure Cases. ‣ Appendix G Tokenizer Analysis ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> summarizes representative errors,
including substitutions of named entities and numerals, as well as errors in
diacritics, usernames, markup, and word boundaries.</p>
</div>
