<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Probing the Hierarchy</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the 12-level TinyStories model.
TinyStories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> contains short, simple stories that make
changes easy to follow. We encode 128-token stories with the VQ-VAE to obtain
codes at each level of the hierarchy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shared Structure, Different Realizations.</span>
We fix the first three levels of the hierarchy and randomly sample the remaining
nine levels using two seeds.
As shown in Figure <a href="#A8.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ Appendix H Probing the Hierarchy ‣ N C M Next-Concept Modeling: Fast, Parallel Coarse-to-Fine Text Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>, the outputs tend to preserve the
broad theme of the source story while varying names, objects, and wording.
Story A retains a girl seeking warmth and a garment that moves; Story B retains
Tim’s alarm at something in the sky.</p>
</div>
